  "full_response": "### **Microservices Architecture**\nMicroservices is an architectural style that structures an application as a collection of **loosely coupled, independently deployable services**. Each service:\n- Has a **single responsibility** (e.g., user authentication, payment processing, inventory management).\n- Runs in its own **process** and communicates via **well-defined APIs** (REST, gRPC, messaging queues).\n- Can be **developed, deployed, and scaled independently**.\n- Often uses **different technologies** (languages, databases) based on needs.\n\n#### **Key Characteristics**\n✔ **Decentralized** – Teams own services end-to-end (DevOps culture).\n✔ **Resilient** – Failure in one service doesn’t crash the entire system.\n✔ **Scalable** – Services can be scaled independently (e.g., only scale the payment service during Black Friday).\n✔ **Easier Maintenance** – Smaller codebases are simpler to update and debug.\n✔ **Technology Flexibility** – Different services can use different stacks (e.g., Python for ML, Go for high-performance APIs).\n\n---\n\n### **Monolithic Architecture**\nA monolith is a **single, unified codebase** where all components (UI, business logic, database access) are **tightly coupled** and deployed as one unit.\n\n#### **Key Characteristics**\n✔ **Simple to Develop** – Single codebase, easier to test locally.\n✔ **Easier Debugging** – All logic is in one place (no distributed tracing needed).\n✔ **Simpler Deployment** – One artifact to deploy (no orchestration complexity).\n✔ **Performance** – In-process communication is faster than network calls.\n✔ **Consistency** – Transactions and data integrity are easier to manage.\n\n---\n\n### **Trade-offs: Microservices vs. Monolithic**\n\n| **Factor**               | **Microservices** | **Monolithic** |\n|--------------------------|------------------|----------------|\n| **Development Speed**    | Slower (distributed complexity) | Faster (simpler setup) |\n| **Scalability**          | High (scale only what’s needed) | Low (must scale entire app) |\n| **Deployment**           | Complex (CI/CD per service) | Simple (single deployment) |\n| **Fault Isolation**      | High (failures are contained) | Low (one bug can crash everything) |\n| **Technology",
